WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.910
One of the worst-kept secrets in all of television
history is the fact that Adult Swim's Rick

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:09.620
and Morty is filled to the brim with Easter
eggs, tongue-in-cheek references, and obvious

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.870
homages to all things pop culture.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:16.939
While co-creators Dan Harmon and Justin Roiland
have imbued the sci-fi adventure comedy series

00:00:16.939 --> 00:00:21.640
with a ton of clear film and television mentions,
they've also incorporated at least double

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:22.939
that amount under the radar.

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:25.929
Here are some references in Rick and Morty
that you may have missed.

00:00:25.929 --> 00:00:28.359
"Rick and Morty and their adventures, Morty!

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:32.529
Rick and Morty forever and forever hundred
years Rick and Morty things!

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:36.699
Me and Rick and Morty runnin’ around and
Rick and Morty time!"

00:00:36.699 --> 00:00:37.699
Community

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:41.230
For fans who've been around the television-watching
block a few times before, it's hardly a shock

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:46.460
to learn that series co-creator Dan Harmon
was the brains behind NBC's Community.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:51.440
Unfortunately, Community had a troubled six-year
run, temporarily losing Harmon as a showrunner

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.110
and switching networks before it was finally
cancelled.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:58.530
In Rick and Morty, Harmon pokes some serious
fun at the downfall of Community, hitting

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.160
the series' soft spots.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.610
In Season 2's "Auto Erotic Assimilation,"
Rick and his on-again and off-again collective

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.400
hivemind lover Unity are watching television,
with the events playing out on-screen controlled

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.400
by Unity itself.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.160
"Now make them all make fun of the blonde
one!

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.570
Now make them all do it on the table!

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.020
I can’t believe you created a whole show
for me.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.020
Now cancel it!

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.000
Ok, now put it back on!"

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.650
And if there was any doubt, the whole crew
appears onscreen a moment later.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.049
Weekend at Bernie's

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:30.869
The 1989 black comedy Weekend at Bernie's
follows a pair of low-level suits working

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:35.240
at a New York insurance company who end up
spending their Labor Day weekend hauling around

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.020
their dead boss, Bernie, and trying to convince
everyone he's still alive.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.900
Classic, right?

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.360
"Ew."

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.030
"Don’t panic!"

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:48.549
Among the bits in season one's "Rixty Minutes,"
there's a spoof trailer for a movie that centers

00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:51.500
around a dead elderly woman being puppeted
by her cats.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.659
"What are the kitties to do, but buckle together
and work as a team."

00:01:55.659 --> 00:02:00.829
"Last Will and Testimeow: Weekend at Dead
Cat Lady’s House 2."

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:02.530
Hail to the King

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:07.100
The season one episode "Something Ricked This
Way Comes" isn't shy about its inspiration.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:13.320
It's a sci-fi mash-up of Ray Bradbury's 1962
dark fantasy novel Something Wicked This Way

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.460
Comes, in which a mysterious man gives the
locals their greatest desires.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:18.530
It's not exactly subtle.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:23.261
"It detects and catalogs all your Twilight
Zone, Ray Bradbury, Friday the 13th the series

00:02:23.261 --> 00:02:24.920
voodoo crap magic."

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.230
It's also an obvious riff on Stephen King's
classic horror book Needful Things, where

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:32.250
an elderly man runs a very weird, dangerous
shop.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.770
But the vintage horror references don't end
there.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.180
"I'm here to pick up my undead cat and child."

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.890
While the quip may just seem like typical
Rick and Morty irreverence, it's actually

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:46.000
a subtle reference to Stephen King's 1983
horror novel Pet Sematary, which follows the

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.120
Creed family.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.000
They're plagued by back-to-back tragedies:
the accidental deaths of their cat and their

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.880
two-year-old son, both of whom disturbingly
come back to life as demonic weirdos determined

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:56.880
to kill.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.840
And the Fourth-Dimensional Testicle Monsters
from "A Rickle in Time"?

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.570
They're a spitting image of the terrifying
time-bending creatures seen in the 1995 mini-series,

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:08.080
The Langoliers, based on a story by Stephen
King.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.200
"You better keep back!

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.820
Man, this will turn your ass into a third
trimester fetus from thirty yards!"

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.320
Zardoz

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.590
Raising Gazorpazorp will likely go down in
Rick and Morty history as one of the strangest

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.090
episodes the series has ever put forth.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:26.410
But with all its uniqueness, there were references
hidden throughout, like the Footloose tribute

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:30.520
that sees Morty Jr. busting an angsty move
in an abandoned warehouse.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.320
And who could miss the Raising Arizona nod
in the title?

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.890
But it goes deeper than that.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:39.240
When Rick and Summer pay a visit to Gazorpazorp
in the Andromeda system, they see a giant

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:44.110
floating head fly in to drop down artificially
crafted women used for procreation.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:49.970
The head is almost identical to the one seen
in the 1974 sci-fi cult film Zardoz, a flick

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:55.910
co-creator Harmon told his Harmontown podcast
listeners to watch back in April 2013.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:57.210
Animal Farm

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:02.120
George Orwell's Animal Farm gets a few carefully
crafted references throughout the "Lawnmower

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.280
Dog" episode of Rick and Morty.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.520
And in case you're counting, that title is
another Stephen King reference.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:12.130
When the family's pet pup Snuffles gains sentience
and decides to take down all of humanity,

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:17.120
he uses his powers to construct a full body
suit and express just how angry he is about

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.549
the way dogs have grown from wild beasts to
humans' playthings.

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:24.550
It's a progression of events that loosely
mimics what's seen in Orwell's allegorical

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:25.550
novella.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:26.990
But that's just surface-level stuff.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:30.229
There's also this scene where Snuffles decides
to change his name.

00:04:30.229 --> 00:04:32.889
"Dad, he wants to be called Snowball."

00:04:32.889 --> 00:04:33.889
"Well, I’m not calling him that.

00:04:33.889 --> 00:04:35.259
That’s ridiculous."

00:04:35.259 --> 00:04:38.949
If you know your literature, Snowball is the
name of one of the main characters in Animal

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:40.169
Farm.

00:04:40.169 --> 00:04:42.289
Buck Rogers in the 25th Century

00:04:42.289 --> 00:04:46.480
There are about a thousand reasons why the
noble Birdperson is such a cherished Rick

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.580
and Morty character, but even his character
is borrowed wholesale from 1980s sci-fi.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:57.120
The TV series Buck Rogers in the 25th Century
had no shortage of interesting characters,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.780
but it was the alien named Hawk who really
stood out.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.600
Hawk, played by Thom Christopher, was a half-man,
half-bird warrior who represented the last

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.729
of a dying breed of bird people; a background
Birdperson himself possesses.

00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:11.939
Hawk and Birdperson are similar in appearance
as well.

00:05:11.939 --> 00:05:15.900
One thing's for certain: we wouldn't put it
past Rick and Morty to weave a web as weird

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.450
as one that links Buck Rogers to their own
multi-dimensional chaos.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:21.849
Aliens aplenty

00:05:21.849 --> 00:05:25.039
Rick and Morty has been riding the reference
train from the get-go.

00:05:25.039 --> 00:05:29.169
Pause the pilot for just a moment to get an
up-close-and-personal look at the visually

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:33.400
complex spaceport sequence, and you'll notice
that a number of the silhouettes shown are

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.610
actually classic pop culture figures.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:40.080
Near the center of the frame, are three creatures
that look exactly like the wisecracking robots

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.360
Tom Servo, Crow T. Robot, and Gypsy from the
sci-fi comedy series Mystery Science Theater

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:46.360
3000.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:51.610
Nearby, there's a shadow that resembles the
terrifying Xenomorph from the Alien film franchise.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:55.499
Way over to the left-hand side of the screen,
there's a carbon copy of the blippy alien

00:05:55.499 --> 00:05:58.350
figure from the 1978 arcade game Space Invaders.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:01.920
And of course, a gratuitous Ferengi from Star
Trek.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:06.900
Gravity Falls

00:06:06.909 --> 00:06:10.740
If you didn't have your peepers peeled, you
may have missed a few quick nods to Disney's

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.939
Gravity Falls, created by Justin Roiland's
friend Alex Hirsch.

00:06:14.939 --> 00:06:19.810
Look closely during the scene in "Close Rick-counters
of the Rick Kind", and you'll see a pen, notepad,

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:24.870
and mug emerge from a portal: the exact same
items Stan drops through a portal in Gravity

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.180
Falls' "Society of the Blind Eye" episode.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.610
And during "Big Trouble in Little Sanchez,"
you can spot an image of Gravity Falls' main

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:34.810
antagonist, Bill Cipher, on an alien computer
screen.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:39.039
Even deeper than that, in the season three
premiere "The Rickshank Rickdemption," a Morty

00:06:39.039 --> 00:06:43.240
is seen holding what appears to be Journal
3 from Gravity Falls as he walks through the

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:44.680
Citadel of Ricks.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:48.969
And with so many off-kilter versions of the
duo in sight, you may not have spotted a set

00:06:48.969 --> 00:06:53.389
of Morty twins in the background: one is wearing
Dipper's pine tree hat, and the other has

00:06:53.389 --> 00:06:56.229
on Mabel's headband and sports her signature
hairstyle.

00:06:56.229 --> 00:06:59.350
"And that's the way the news goes."

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:00.690
Thanks for watching!

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.449
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:03.449 --> 00:07:05.699
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

